{"name":"Montego Bay","hex":"#3fbabd","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Montego Bay","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A city, the parish capital of Saint James parish, Jamaica."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Montego_Bay"}],"entries":[{"title":"Montego Bay","blocks":[{"text":"Montego Bay is the capital of the parish of St. James in Jamaica. The city is the fourth most populous urban area in the country, after Kingston, Spanish Town, and Portmore, all of which form the Greater Kingston Metropolitan Area, home to more than half a million people. As a result, Montego Bay is the second-largest anglophone city in the Caribbean, after Kingston."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Montego_Bay_Photo_Don_Ramey_Logan.jpg/330px-Montego_Bay_Photo_Don_Ramey_Logan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Montego_Bay","wikidata":"Q637555"},{"title":"Montego Bay (song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Montego Bay\" is a song co-written and performed by Bobby Bloom about the city in Jamaica of the same name. The song was a top ten hit for Bloom in the Fall of 1970 on both sides of the Atlantic. It reached No. 3 on the UK Singles Chart, No. 5 on the Canadian RPM 100 Singles Chart, No. 7 on the Australian Go-Set Singles Chart and No. 8 on the US Billboard Hot 100. The song was co-written and produced by Jeff Barry. In the master tape of the song, Bloom breaks into a chorus of \"Oh, What a Beautiful Mornin'\" at the end of the recording. The song features a whistler, as well as Jamaican instruments in a calypso style."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/6/6a/Montego_Bay_-_Bobby_Bloom.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Montego_Bay_(song)","wikidata":"Q10588770"},{"title":"Bay","blocks":[{"text":"A bay is a recessed, coastal body of water that directly connects to a larger main body of water, such as an ocean, a lake, or another bay. A large bay is usually called a gulf, sea, sound, or bight. A cove is a small, circular bay with a narrow entrance. A fjord is an elongated bay formed by glacial action.\nThe term embayment is also used for related features, such as extinct bays or freshwater environments."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Aerial_photograph_of_Langness_and_the_bay_at_Castletown_13_February_2014_-_geograph.org.uk_-_3847162.jpg/330px-Aerial_photograph_of_Langness_and_the_bay_at_Castletown_13_February_2014_-_geograph.org.uk_-_3847162.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bay","wikidata":"Q39594"},{"title":"Bay (horse)","blocks":[{"text":"Bay is a hair coat color of horses, characterized by a reddish-brown or brown body color with a black point coloration on the mane, tail, ear edges, and lower legs. Bay is one of the most common coat colors in many horse breeds."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/BayMare.jpg/330px-BayMare.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bay_(horse)","wikidata":"Q900906"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Hakewill%2C_A_Picturesque_Tour_of_the_Island_of_Jamaica%2C_Plate_18.jpg/500px-Hakewill%2C_A_Picturesque_Tour_of_the_Island_of_Jamaica%2C_Plate_18.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Hakewill%2C_A_Picturesque_Tour_of_the_Island_of_Jamaica%2C_Plate_18.jpg/1280px-Hakewill%2C_A_Picturesque_Tour_of_the_Island_of_Jamaica%2C_Plate_18.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Hakewill%2C_A_Picturesque_Tour_of_the_Island_of_Jamaica%2C_Plate_18.jpg/500px-Hakewill%2C_A_Picturesque_Tour_of_the_Island_of_Jamaica%2C_Plate_18.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Montego Bay, c. 1820","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHakewill%2C_A_Picturesque_Tour_of_the_Island_of_Jamaica%2C_Plate_18.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Doctors-Cave-Beach.jpg/500px-Doctors-Cave-Beach.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Doctors-Cave-Beach.jpg/500px-Doctors-Cave-Beach.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Doctor's Cave Beach Club","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADoctors-Cave-Beach.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/JM-montego-bay-nat-mus.jpg/500px-JM-montego-bay-nat-mus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/JM-montego-bay-nat-mus.jpg/1280px-JM-montego-bay-nat-mus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/JM-montego-bay-nat-mus.jpg/500px-JM-montego-bay-nat-mus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Montego Bay Cultural Centre. The building was formerly a ballroom and courthouse during the height of the country's colonial period. It is also home to the National Gallery of Jamaica West.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJM-montego-bay-nat-mus.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Montego_bay-1001.jpg/500px-Montego_bay-1001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Montego_bay-1001.jpg/1280px-Montego_bay-1001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campa